{"name":"Golden Poppy","hex":"#fcc200","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"golden poppy","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Synonym of California poppy."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/golden_poppy"}],"entries":[{"title":"Eschscholzia californica","blocks":[{"text":"Eschscholzia californica, the California poppy, golden poppy, Mexican poppy, California sunlight or cup of gold, is a species of flowering plant in the family Papaveraceae, native to the United States and Mexico. It is cultivated as an ornamental plant flowering in summer, with showy flowers in brilliant shades of red, orange and yellow. It is also used as food or a garnish. It had various uses in indigenous herbalism. It became the official state flower of California in 1903."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/Kaldari_Eschscholzia_californica_01.jpg/330px-Kaldari_Eschscholzia_californica_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eschscholzia_californica","wikidata":"Q158795"},{"title":"Poppy","blocks":[{"text":"A poppy is a flowering plant in the subfamily Papaveroideae of the family Papaveraceae. Poppies are herbaceous plants, often grown for their colourful flowers. One species of poppy, Papaver somniferum, is the source of the narcotic drug mixture opium, which contains powerful medicinal alkaloids such as morphine and has been used since ancient times as an analgesic and narcotic medicinal and recreational drug. It also produces edible seeds. Following the trench warfare in the poppy fields of Flanders, Belgium, during World War I, poppies, specifically the red poppy, have become a symbol of remembrance of soldiers who have died during wartime, especially in the UK, Canada, Australia, Africa and other Commonwealth realms."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/71/Poppies_in_the_Sunset_on_Lake_Geneva.jpg/330px-Poppies_in_